= Persistent state contamination (artificial intelligence) — Simple =

'''Persistent state contamination''' happens when bad information is saved into an AI agent's durable state and later reused as if it were trustworthy.

For example:

<pre>
agent makes a wrong inference
→ saves it as memory
→ later retrieves the memory
→ acts on the old mistake
</pre>

The bad state can come from an accident, stale information, wrong identity binding, or a deliberate attack.

'''Memory poisoning''' is one adversarial version of this problem. Persistent state contamination is broader because it also includes ordinary mistakes that become durable.

Useful protections include provenance, scope labels, validation before writes, versioning, quarantine, and rollback.

